								<div class="title" id="post-title-2159833">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/chainsawmissus" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2008358); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2008358&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1610118795" alt="chainsawmissus" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2008358)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/chainsawmissus" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2008358); return false;" >chainsawmissus</a></span> <span class="odate time_1416281088 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Nov 2014 03:24</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2159833">
									

<p>I am not only not clear on what is going on here, but on what things actually happened and in what order.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">First,&nbsp;a&nbsp;few&nbsp;nitpicks</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;I&nbsp;wouldn't&nbsp;say&nbsp;it&nbsp;if&nbsp;I&nbsp;didn't&nbsp;care</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>If the milk bottles refilled themselves when placed on the porch, aren't the bottles themselves anomalous and should have been taken? The last addendum makes it sound like they did but I didn't see that in the old containment procedures.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>However, the Second Life Labor Service is also to be implemented</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What the heck is this? It is never brought up again.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The official cover story during containment is to establish that the area is currently undergoing sensitive relations with Native American peoples. Similarly, those who attempt to leave Safety Harbor are to be deterred with the news that nearby areas are undergoing conflict with Native American peoples.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That hardly feels like a reason to keep people from entering or leaving town. &quot;I'm sorry sir, you can't pass this checkpoint. It might upset the Seminole tribe.&quot; I like the feel of the town adapting, but it might make more sense in a remote area. How long can they keep people from entering or leaving town? While we are on the subject, how long did the re-animations keep going for? You made it sound like the milk bottle exchange ended in 1939.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The meeting transcript implies that this was the first encounter with the anomaly related to SCP-2488.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Does this mean it is just their first contact with the re-animations? Unless somebody drank the mystery milk for breakfast and dropped dead into his eggs before the next meeting, they would all have been familiar with the magic milk bottles.</p>
<p>DOCUMENT 2488&#160;17-C doesn't seem to contribute anything. You introduced the church in the document above it as being pro milk-zombie. Now you are saying there is dissent but it doesn't go anywhere.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This recovered documentation was discovered as investigation into SCP-2488 was initiated.<br />
It was, as the mayor said, a storage space full of heritage documents that they didn't dare to go through, lest they damage or lose any of them.<br />
The mayor couldn't tell us much, other than that it was some old folklore rolling around the town.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>These notes make it look like the Foundation explored the phenomenon through the documents they found but earlier it sounded like they found it right after the newspaper clippings about the re-animations.</p>
<p>This is followed up by the last record which is clearly a modern view looking into an event that is over.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The anomaly only took a few hours to discover.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What does this mean? The new milk bottles? The radiation?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Maybe&nbsp;I&nbsp;am&nbsp;thinking&nbsp;about&nbsp;his&nbsp;too&nbsp;much</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;I&nbsp;have&nbsp;had&nbsp;a&nbsp;few&nbsp;days&nbsp;to&nbsp;stew&nbsp;over&nbsp;it.</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>There is a lot of this that I can't seem to make any sense out of. The first and probably dumbest one is: why milk? Before the importation of cattle, horses, and goats Native Americans did not have any milk-producing livestock and like other populations with no dairy to eat they would probably have been largely lactose intolerant.</p>
<p>It is possible that a milk bottle is one of the pieces of litter that was left behind on the mound and maybe that had something to do with it.</p>
<p>Finally, I don't understand the manual labor bit. A re-animated corpse is good for 3 to 5 weeks. The average human lives about seventy years and works for about 50. There can't be more than one active corpse for every 750 able bodied people. How helpful can that be?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
Things I did like: the eulogy, the fact the someone else covered up the newspaper story, the church helping to release the corpses, and the milk bottle in the mound. There is a strong sense of history here, but I have to downvote over the confused timeline and self-contradictions.
							</div>

								
					
